1
00:00:29,280 --> 00:00:31,574
Hey, howdy there, Mr. Lee.

2
00:00:31,741 --> 00:00:34,035
What is this? Filipino New Year's?

3
00:00:34,577 --> 00:00:36,037
No. Why you say that?

4
00:00:36,204 --> 00:00:37,872
Because you're cooking up a feast.

5
00:00:38,289 --> 00:00:40,667
I can smell it all the way
from the interstate.

6
00:00:40,833 --> 00:00:43,294
What is that? Barbecue?

7
00:00:43,461 --> 00:00:46,256
Oh, yeah. But it not coming from here.

8
00:00:46,798 --> 00:00:48,424
What is that noise?

9
00:00:54,180 --> 00:00:55,974
Daddy!

10
00:01:00,979 --> 00:01:03,189
- Daddy!
- Daddy!

11
00:04:19,927 --> 00:04:22,805
What is your take on this, Jerry?

12
00:04:22,972 --> 00:04:25,683
The people are gonna love it,
Mr. President.

13
00:04:25,850 --> 00:04:30,396
Our only conflict is whether we ambush
the 6:00 news or hold out for prime time.

14
00:04:30,563 --> 00:04:32,940
Screw the press.
This is a need-to-know situation.

15
00:04:33,107 --> 00:04:34,650
We should make it top secret…

16
00:04:34,817 --> 00:04:36,986
… and move in right away
with DEFCON 4.

17
00:04:37,153 --> 00:04:39,113
Mr. President, we cannot sit on this.

18
00:04:40,990 --> 00:04:44,368
General Casey, do you have an opinion?

19
00:04:44,535 --> 00:04:47,288
Well, sir, do we know they're hostile?

20
00:04:47,455 --> 00:04:49,874
What do you mean,
"do we know they're hostile?"

21
00:04:50,041 --> 00:04:52,752
They have our planet surrounded
with thousands of warships.

22
00:04:52,919 --> 00:04:54,462
Do we know they're warships?

23
00:04:58,382 --> 00:05:01,135
Professor, what do we know about them?

24
00:05:02,678 --> 00:05:05,097
We know they're extremely advanced
technologically…

25
00:05:05,264 --> 00:05:08,309
… which suggests, very rightfully so,
that they're peaceful.

26
00:05:09,185 --> 00:05:13,231
An advanced civilization is,
by definition, not barbaric.

27
00:05:13,397 --> 00:05:16,526
Mr. President, this is a great day.

28
00:05:17,068 --> 00:05:20,488
I and all my colleagues
are extremely excited.

29
00:05:21,405 --> 00:05:23,032
Good.

30
00:05:23,574 --> 00:05:26,410
Extraterrestrial life.

31
00:05:27,078 --> 00:05:28,579
You're right, Jerry.

32
00:05:28,746 --> 00:05:31,082
The people are gonna love it.

33
00:05:31,249 --> 00:05:33,709
This is a momentous occasion.

34
00:05:34,502 --> 00:05:37,296
- Mr. President, we have to act—
- We'll go all media on this.

35
00:05:37,880 --> 00:05:42,218
I'll wear my blue Cerruti suit.
And, Jerry, I'll need a good speech.

36
00:05:42,385 --> 00:05:43,469
Statesman-like…

37
00:05:44,053 --> 00:05:47,932
… historical and yet warm and neighborly.

38
00:05:48,099 --> 00:05:51,894
Abraham Lincoln meets <i>Leave It to Beaver.</i>
You know the sort of thing. Heh.

39
00:05:52,061 --> 00:05:53,187
Yes, sir.

40
00:05:53,354 --> 00:05:55,106
- Are they too old-fashioned?
- Yes.

41
00:05:55,273 --> 00:05:56,732
No, I don't think so.

42
00:05:56,899 --> 00:06:00,361
This has got the colors, but no.
I don't think so.

43
00:06:00,528 --> 00:06:02,071
- Not vivid enough.
- Mm-hm. Mm-hm.

44
00:06:02,238 --> 00:06:04,866
- Nancy had this in the library, didn't she?
- Yes, indeed.

45
00:06:05,032 --> 00:06:06,158
I don't think so.

46
00:06:06,325 --> 00:06:09,954
I thought perhaps the watered silk.
And this week it's on sale.

47
00:06:10,121 --> 00:06:12,748
I hardly think I need worry about that.

48
00:06:12,915 --> 00:06:15,585
My husband is the leader
of the free world.

49
00:06:16,794 --> 00:06:18,880
What do you think, Taffy?

50
00:06:19,046 --> 00:06:22,633
Why don't you leave the Roosevelt Room
the way the Roosevelts wanted it?

51
00:06:23,968 --> 00:06:27,972
Because Eleanor Roosevelt
was too fond of chintz. That's why.

52
00:06:28,306 --> 00:06:30,433
Mother, this isn't your house.

53
00:06:30,600 --> 00:06:33,686
Taffy, if you're going to be a pest,
I'm going to ignore you.

54
00:06:34,228 --> 00:06:36,606
- Mrs. Dale?
- Yes?

55
00:06:57,501 --> 00:07:01,422
- I saw you fight Sonny Liston in '69.
- Really?

56
00:07:01,589 --> 00:07:03,841
- You mean, you were a nun back then?
- Oh, yes.

57
00:07:04,008 --> 00:07:07,053
We've always been fight fans,
haven't we, sisters?

58
00:07:07,219 --> 00:07:09,096
<i>Byron Williams, telephone.</i>

59
00:07:09,263 --> 00:07:11,223
<i>- Byron Williams…</i>
- That's me. I gotta go.

60
00:07:11,390 --> 00:07:13,935
You ladies have a nice time, all right?
Thank you.

61
00:07:18,064 --> 00:07:19,398
- Yeah?
<i>- Byron?</i>

62
00:07:19,565 --> 00:07:20,608
Oh, Louise.

63
00:07:21,067 --> 00:07:23,152
I'm sorry I have to call you at work.

64
00:07:23,903 --> 00:07:27,949
The boys haven't been home in two nights
and I don't know what to do.

65
00:07:28,407 --> 00:07:30,201
Listen, you're doing the best you can.

66
00:07:30,409 --> 00:07:32,161
<i>They're just at that age.</i>

67
00:07:34,205 --> 00:07:37,291
Louise, the casino manager's
giving me the evil eye. I've gotta go.

68
00:07:37,959 --> 00:07:39,669
<i>I'll call you later, okay?</i>

69
00:07:40,628 --> 00:07:43,631
Hey, are you still cool on me
coming to Washington?

70
00:07:43,798 --> 00:07:45,466
Sure. Of course I am.

71
00:07:46,175 --> 00:07:49,220
You take care, okay? Bye.

72
00:08:02,817 --> 00:08:04,110
Ahh…

73
00:08:04,443 --> 00:08:06,237
Do you have to drink in front of me?

74
00:08:06,404 --> 00:08:08,531
You're an adult. Just cope.

75
00:08:08,698 --> 00:08:11,826
It doesn't help me
that all we ever do is sit around in bars.

76
00:08:11,993 --> 00:08:14,120
Hey. This is work, baby, okay?

77
00:08:14,286 --> 00:08:17,707
I'm checking the decor,
the lighting, the traffic flow.

78
00:08:17,873 --> 00:08:21,085
If I'd known you'd turn into a crook,
I never would have married you.

79
00:08:21,252 --> 00:08:22,795
I'm not a crook.

80
00:08:22,962 --> 00:08:25,339
I'm ambitious. There's a difference.

81
00:08:25,506 --> 00:08:28,092
And if you think you can make
a nickel in this town…

82
00:08:28,259 --> 00:08:32,138
… without knowing how to dally
around a few curves, well…

83
00:08:32,304 --> 00:08:34,932
… you don't know doodlysquat
about the gaming industry.

84
00:08:35,099 --> 00:08:36,308
Oh, Art.

85
00:08:36,475 --> 00:08:40,438
Galaxy's gonna be
the best hotel in Vegas.

86
00:08:40,604 --> 00:08:43,024
The best. I promise.

87
00:08:43,190 --> 00:08:45,985
Don't you realize what you're doing?
Destroying the Earth.

88
00:08:46,152 --> 00:08:49,030
All this greed. This money system.

89
00:08:49,196 --> 00:08:50,906
You're destroying everything.

90
00:08:51,073 --> 00:08:53,993
Okay, okay, Barbara. Okay.

91
00:08:54,368 --> 00:08:56,829
Keep your voice down.

92
00:08:57,371 --> 00:08:59,665
I got friends here.

93
00:09:00,791 --> 00:09:03,252
Sugar. Hit me one more time.

94
00:09:03,419 --> 00:09:06,213
- Stop flirting with the waitress.
- Oh, God. Here.

95
00:09:07,965 --> 00:09:09,592
Go on over to the roulette…

96
00:09:11,260 --> 00:09:12,803
… play our anniversary…

97
00:09:12,970 --> 00:09:15,097
… and stay off of black.

98
00:09:18,309 --> 00:09:19,393
Thanks, honey.

99
00:09:26,233 --> 00:09:27,485
Yeah?

100
00:09:27,651 --> 00:09:30,654
Make it quick.
We go out live in 10 minutes.

101
00:09:31,280 --> 00:09:34,825
What? The actual president?
What do you mean, "cutting in"?

102
00:09:39,121 --> 00:09:41,999
<i>Budget negotiations may be dead
in the water after this week.</i>

103
00:09:42,750 --> 00:09:44,668
Hair looks good.

104
00:09:45,336 --> 00:09:46,378
I like the hair.

105
00:09:46,545 --> 00:09:48,297
<i>—quote, "factually challenged."
Griffin says…</i>

106
00:09:49,673 --> 00:09:51,342
Yeah, Stone. Speak.

107
00:09:51,509 --> 00:09:52,968
Jason, hi, it's me.

108
00:09:54,261 --> 00:09:55,930
You wearing a bra?

109
00:09:56,097 --> 00:09:59,767
Listen, this is big. President Dale
is cutting into my show today.

110
00:10:01,352 --> 00:10:04,355
That's absurd. Why would he stoop
to being on <i>Today in Fashion?</i>

111
00:10:04,522 --> 00:10:05,856
He's interrupting everybody.

112
00:10:06,023 --> 00:10:09,401
I don't know, it's some sort of
emergency announcement or something.

113
00:10:09,568 --> 00:10:11,987
This doesn't make sense.
He should be talking to us.

114
00:10:13,531 --> 00:10:15,825
White House is coming out live.

115
00:10:16,450 --> 00:10:19,161
Good evening, my fellow Americans.

116
00:10:19,787 --> 00:10:23,040
I apologize for interrupting
your regular programs…

117
00:10:23,207 --> 00:10:26,335
… but I have a very important
announcement to make.

118
00:10:27,044 --> 00:10:29,880
<i>Many important things have happened
to me in my life:</i>

119
00:10:30,673 --> 00:10:32,675
<i>My graduation from Princeton…</i>

120
00:10:33,634 --> 00:10:36,512
… the day that Marsha said
she would be my wife…

121
00:10:36,679 --> 00:10:38,764
… the birth of our daughter, Taffy.

122
00:10:38,931 --> 00:10:39,974
Thanks, Dad.

123
00:10:40,558 --> 00:10:42,726
<i>And the news that I have heard today…</i>

124
00:10:42,893 --> 00:10:45,020
<i>- … ranks right up there.</i>
- Thank you.

125
00:10:45,771 --> 00:10:48,107
<i>A powerful memory is in the making.</i>

126
00:10:48,274 --> 00:10:49,900
<i>Not just for me…</i>

127
00:10:50,067 --> 00:10:52,069
<i>… but for all mankind.</i>

128
00:10:52,236 --> 00:10:57,950
<i>Today, an extraordinary discovery
was made by the Hubble telescope.</i>

129
00:10:58,993 --> 00:11:02,705
<i>The data from the Hubble was decoded…</i>

130
00:11:02,872 --> 00:11:04,915
<i>… then analyzed
by the most powerful computers at MIT.</i>

131
00:11:08,085 --> 00:11:10,296
<i>The images are undeniable.</i>

132
00:11:10,963 --> 00:11:14,300
<i>We are entering the dawn of a new era.</i>

133
00:11:15,259 --> 00:11:18,929
<i>Frame enlargements
provide an astonishing sight.</i>

134
00:11:19,597 --> 00:11:24,101
<i>A fleet of vehicles,
which can best be described as…</i>

135
00:11:24,268 --> 00:11:25,644
<i>… flying saucers.</i>

136
00:11:25,811 --> 00:11:29,481
Come on. Come on.
Papa needs a new pair of shoes.

137
00:11:29,648 --> 00:11:31,525
Hey!

138
00:11:32,109 --> 00:11:34,153
Hey, am I the only one
shooting craps here?

139
00:11:34,320 --> 00:11:37,740
<i>—we will have
the opportunity to meet with them.</i>

140
00:11:37,907 --> 00:11:41,076
<i>I feel this is the perfect summation
to the 20th century…</i>

141
00:11:41,243 --> 00:11:43,287
Martians.

142
00:11:43,454 --> 00:11:44,914
This is great.

143
00:11:46,165 --> 00:11:48,667
Please come to Earth. Please.

144
00:11:49,293 --> 00:11:50,586
We need you.

145
00:11:54,965 --> 00:11:57,134
I got people coming in.

146
00:11:57,301 --> 00:12:00,304
Everybody wants to be a part of this.

147
00:12:00,471 --> 00:12:03,349
Galaxy's gonna be world-class.

148
00:12:03,807 --> 00:12:05,142
<i>And soon…</i>

149
00:12:05,309 --> 00:12:08,354
<i>… we will become one solar system.</i>

150
00:12:08,896 --> 00:12:10,689
What the hell is he talking about?

151
00:12:10,856 --> 00:12:14,318
<i>These flying saucers
have come from the planet Mars…</i>

152
00:12:14,485 --> 00:12:17,696
<i>- … and at their current course and speed…</i>
- Oh. Hey.

153
00:12:17,863 --> 00:12:23,452
Listen, Hoss. I've been thinking about
Martians when there wasn't no Martians.

154
00:12:23,744 --> 00:12:28,332
<i>It is profoundly moving
to know there is intelligent life out there.</i>

155
00:12:28,499 --> 00:12:29,792
Glad they got it somewhere.

156
00:12:29,959 --> 00:12:34,338
And our world will never feel
quite the same again.

157
00:12:35,547 --> 00:12:37,299
Good night…

158
00:12:37,841 --> 00:12:39,843
… and God bless you all.

159
00:12:50,104 --> 00:12:51,939
This…

160
00:12:52,314 --> 00:12:53,524
… is intense.

161
00:12:56,819 --> 00:12:58,696
Shut up, Poppy. Shut up.

162
00:12:59,697 --> 00:13:01,532
Shut up.

163
00:13:01,865 --> 00:13:03,701
Jason Stone.

164
00:13:06,036 --> 00:13:07,705
It's for you.

165
00:13:08,872 --> 00:13:11,417
Poppy, honey. No, sweetie.

166
00:13:13,460 --> 00:13:17,214
That's right. Shh. Good girl. Yeah?

167
00:13:18,132 --> 00:13:21,510
Yeah. Well, I could be there at…

168
00:13:21,677 --> 00:13:23,470
… I don't know, 11.

169
00:13:24,430 --> 00:13:26,598
Okay, bye-bye.

170
00:13:36,567 --> 00:13:38,068
Yes?

171
00:13:38,944 --> 00:13:42,156
Oh, they want me to go interview
that professor from the White House.

172
00:13:43,157 --> 00:13:44,908
Kessler?

173
00:13:45,951 --> 00:13:48,245
- Donald Kessler?
- Yeah, I think that was his name.

174
00:13:48,412 --> 00:13:51,081
You know, the science guy.

175
00:13:51,248 --> 00:13:53,459
This is nuts, this is…

176
00:13:53,625 --> 00:13:55,127
We should have got that guy.

177
00:13:56,086 --> 00:13:59,048
Well, I can't help it
if your people are too slow.

178
00:13:59,214 --> 00:14:00,257
Right, Poppy?

179
00:14:24,281 --> 00:14:27,284
- Finished!
- One minute, 57 seconds.

180
00:14:27,618 --> 00:14:30,954
- Ha-ha! Didn't I tell you under two minutes?
- You did.

181
00:14:31,121 --> 00:14:33,874
You did. I'm so proud of you.

182
00:14:34,041 --> 00:14:36,710
- Hey, Ma, you want a doughnut?
- How old are they?

183
00:14:36,877 --> 00:14:38,003
Fresh-baked Monday.

184
00:14:38,587 --> 00:14:41,465
Richie, that's six days ago.

185
00:14:41,632 --> 00:14:42,925
Okay, give me a couple.

186
00:14:46,637 --> 00:14:49,098
Hey, this Martian thing
is pretty awesome, huh?

187
00:14:49,264 --> 00:14:52,976
Did any one of you traitors
see my Muffy?

188
00:14:53,143 --> 00:14:54,853
Your brother's gonna volunteer.

189
00:14:55,020 --> 00:14:57,147
As soon as I get back to the base.

190
00:14:57,314 --> 00:15:00,109
- Volunteer for what, Billy Glenn?
- Martian detail.

191
00:15:00,275 --> 00:15:01,944
- Uh-huh.
- Cool.

192
00:15:02,111 --> 00:15:04,113
If any of them Martians
come around here…

193
00:15:04,279 --> 00:15:06,407
… I'm gonna kick their butts.
Heh-heh-heh.

194
00:15:18,127 --> 00:15:19,670
Jump!

195
00:15:28,720 --> 00:15:31,056
Excuse me, folks,
but we gotta make an unscheduled stop.

196
00:15:34,518 --> 00:15:36,353
It's Mama.

197
00:15:36,520 --> 00:15:39,690
What are you doing here? Huh?
If you ain't home, why ain't you in class?

198
00:15:39,857 --> 00:15:42,401
- Mama, class was canceled.
- Class was not canceled.

199
00:15:42,568 --> 00:15:44,153
- Come here. Come here.
- For what?

200
00:15:44,319 --> 00:15:46,071
I'm not gonna hurt you.
Now, come here.

201
00:15:46,238 --> 00:15:48,031
Let go of him.

202
00:15:48,699 --> 00:15:51,076
Okay. Now, just come here.

203
00:15:51,660 --> 00:15:53,412
Come here.

204
00:15:54,079 --> 00:15:55,539
Think it's smart to cut school?

205
00:15:55,706 --> 00:15:58,208
- What are you doing?
- Do you? Do you? Huh? Huh?

206
00:15:58,375 --> 00:15:59,668
- No.
- "No, Mama." It's dumb.

207
00:15:59,835 --> 00:16:01,837
- You're gonna flunk and go to jail.
- No.

208
00:16:02,004 --> 00:16:05,424
Get your butt on the bus.
Get on that bus, boy.

209
00:16:05,591 --> 00:16:07,718
I'll be tripping all over you.
Get back there.

210
00:16:07,885 --> 00:16:10,554
- I don't want to hear another word.
- I hate school.

211
00:16:10,721 --> 00:16:12,639
I heard that.

212
00:16:35,370 --> 00:16:36,914
- Hi.
- Hi there.

213
00:16:37,080 --> 00:16:40,042
- Want a date?
- How about it?

214
00:16:40,209 --> 00:16:41,835
A date?

215
00:16:43,795 --> 00:16:44,838
Sounds good.

216
00:16:47,466 --> 00:16:49,009
The stress at work is…

217
00:16:49,510 --> 00:16:51,553
… unbelievable.

218
00:16:56,433 --> 00:16:58,268
Mr. Bava, can I speak to you in private?

219
00:16:58,435 --> 00:17:01,396
No, I gotta watch the floor.
What do you want, Byron?

220
00:17:01,563 --> 00:17:05,025
- I'm supporting my family back East.
- I thought you were divorced.

221
00:17:05,609 --> 00:17:08,946
Well, I am,
but I still have to take care of them.

222
00:17:09,112 --> 00:17:11,990
Anyway, look. Things have been
a little lean around here…

223
00:17:12,157 --> 00:17:14,368
… and I was wondering
if I could get a raise?

224
00:17:14,535 --> 00:17:17,454
- Out of the question.
- Mr. Bava, I'm an asset to this hotel.

225
00:17:17,621 --> 00:17:20,582
- I mean, they like me around here.
- Byron, you're a nice guy.

226
00:17:21,166 --> 00:17:24,711
But I can get Leon Spinks or Buster Douglas
for the same money, maybe less.

227
00:17:25,254 --> 00:17:28,423
You better get moving.
You're on in five minutes.

228
00:17:37,808 --> 00:17:40,394
This show's gonna get
a hell of a rating.

229
00:17:40,602 --> 00:17:41,687
It's all yours, Ian.

230
00:17:41,853 --> 00:17:44,773
—language you choose to employ,
and in certain circumstances…

231
00:17:44,940 --> 00:17:47,651
… the proposition that two and two
equals five…

232
00:17:47,818 --> 00:17:49,653
… is entirely legitimate.

233
00:17:51,572 --> 00:17:52,614
Call me Nathalie.

234
00:17:53,323 --> 00:17:55,993
And you, do please call me Donald.

235
00:17:57,494 --> 00:17:59,580
I've always admired your show.

236
00:18:01,456 --> 00:18:03,875
Really? You like my work?

237
00:18:04,543 --> 00:18:06,169
Yes. Very much.

238
00:18:06,336 --> 00:18:08,088
Why…

239
00:18:08,338 --> 00:18:10,048
Why, thank you.

240
00:18:10,215 --> 00:18:11,883
Coming out of commercial.

241
00:18:12,050 --> 00:18:15,971
In five, four, three, two…

242
00:18:16,138 --> 00:18:21,018
Welcome back. We're speaking
with Professor Donald Kessler.

243
00:18:21,184 --> 00:18:27,399
He's the chairman
of the American Academy of Astronautics.

244
00:18:28,108 --> 00:18:29,443
Professor…

245
